  "checkpoint": "22E8",
  "clips_scored": 2400,
  "contract_id": "speech_sound_patterns_reference_variety_probe_v1",
  "declared_confounds": [
    "Recording quality varies across Common Voice contributors. Comparing accent subsets of one release, on one platform, from one prompt pool, under one validation process removes most of it, and a difference between two references on the same audio is far better evidence than a difference between two corpora.",
    "Both American subsets are filtered to a declared gender while the Australian and British subsets are not, so American contributors who declared none are absent where the others' are present.",
    "The British subset pools England, Scotland, Wales and Ireland, so it is broader than the variety the repaired reference describes.",
    "Self reported accent is context, not phonetic truth. A speaker tagged Australian may not speak a broad Australian variety.",
    "The dictionaries describe documented varieties and not individual speakers, so a flag may reflect a legitimate personal or regional variant rather than the group's variety.",
    "The aligner writes conditioned palatal allophones as separate phones, so a vowel difference between the varieties can surface as a consonant difference. The secondary analysis excluding that series is how this is checked rather than assumed away."
  ],
  "findings": {
    "group_level_prediction_failed_for_australian_speakers": {
      "australian_differential_under_the_american_reference": -0.003515,
      "statement": "At group level the American reference did not flag Australian speakers more often than American speakers. The differential is negative at every reported threshold, before and after the phone mapping correction, so the checkpoint's central prediction is recorded as wrong rather than reinterpreted."
    },
    "group_level_prediction_held_for_british_speakers": {
      "british_differential_under_the_american_reference": 0.011515,
      "british_differential_under_the_british_reference": 0.005256,
      "statement": "British speakers were flagged more often than the American control under the American reference, and the repaired reference roughly halved that gap. This is the informative middle case behaving as predicted, because it is the variety the repaired reference actually describes."
    },
    "no_detection_accuracy_claim": "These are native speakers reading known text, so a flag is presumed a false concern. Nothing here establishes that the system correctly detects a genuine Australian mispronunciation, and no accuracy, sensitivity or specificity figure is derivable from it.",
    "the_group_mean_hid_a_real_per_consonant_effect": {
      "rhotic_australian_minus_american_under_the_american_reference": 0.030039,
      "rhotic_australian_minus_american_under_the_british_reference": -0.007984,
      "statement": "On the consonants where the varieties actually differ, the predicted penalty is present and large. Under the American reference Australian speakers were flagged more often than American speakers on the rhotic and on t, and both differences collapse under the repaired reference. A mean taken across roughly thirty consonants cannot see an effect carried by three of them, so the checkpoint's hypothesis was sound and its headline statistic was not.",
      "t_australian_minus_american_under_the_american_reference": 0.029779,
      "t_australian_minus_american_under_the_british_reference": 0.00194
    },
    "the_repair_removes_opportunities_rather_than_improving_fit": {
      "american_control_change_under_the_repair": -0.028563,
      "rhotic_flag_rate_for_the_american_control": {
        "american_reference": 0.305598,
        "british_reference": 0.044129
      },
      "rhotic_opportunities_by_reference": {
        "american": {
          "american": 1947,
          "british": 1337
        },
        "australian": {
          "american": 1013,
          "british": 664
        },
        "british": {
          "american": 1047,
          "british": 721
        }
      },
      "statement": "The repaired reference lowers the flag rate in every group, including the American control it should have left alone. The reason is visible in the rhotic: a non-rhotic reference stops expecting a coda r for everybody, so the opportunity is not scored rather than scored better. That is legitimate under this project's own rule, because where varieties genuinely differ the opportunity is unscorable and a mismatch may be excluded but never subtracted. It is not evidence that the reference now describes Australian speakers more accurately, and no such claim is made."
    }
  },
